<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/intermediate/instruments-and-timbre">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Pitch tells you <em>which</em> note; <strong>timbre tells you <em>what</em> is playing it</strong>
    — the colour of sound, built from overtones, attack and resonance. Timbre is the least
    theorised musical element and the most recognisable: it is how you know your mother's voice,
    a Stradivarius from a violin-app, and every synth patch in every song you love.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the anatomy of a sound — overtones, attacks, and the designed recipe" widget="equationStep" config={timbreCfg} />

  <h2>How instruments make their colours</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strings:</strong> bowed (violin: continuous friction, rich sustained overtones), plucked (guitar: bright attack, fast decay), struck (piano: hammer, complex decay). Same string physics, three timbres from the energy input.</li>
    <li><strong>Winds:</strong> air column vibrating — flute (pure, few overtones), clarinet (odd harmonics via reed), brass (lip buzz + flare; the mute is a filter). Material and shape tune the overtone recipe.</li>
    <li><strong>Percussion:</strong> inharmonic overtones (drums, bells) — the maths of a struck membrane is messier than a string, which is why pitched percussion (marimba) is specially tuned.</li>
    <li><strong>The room is part of the instrument:</strong> concert halls, cathedrals and headphones each reshape timbre — concert hall design is applied overtone engineering. (The sound-and-music lesson covers the physics; this is its instrument chapter.)</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Rossing, The Science of Sound; Backus, The Acoustical Foundations of Music; Fletcher & Rossing, The Physics of Musical Instruments.</p>
</TopicPage>
